# SQL changes are numbered files

`supabase/schema.sql` is the baseline the user has already run. Never edit or replace it, or any numbered file after it.

Every new SQL change goes in the next numbered file in `supabase/`, padded to three digits with a short name:

```
supabase/schema.sql          baseline, leave as is
supabase/001_chat_reads.sql  first change
supabase/002_deck_limits.sql next change
```

- Each file holds only that change: `alter table`, `create or replace function`, new grants/revokes. Don't copy the whole schema.
- Make it safe to run on top of everything before it (`if not exists`, `create or replace`, `drop ... if exists`).
- Tell the user the file name to run in the Supabase SQL editor.
